---
/** How Verve reads your day beyond workout minutes. Rendered by /science/movement/movement-balance; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
import MovementBalance from "../../components/science/MovementBalance.astro";
import {
  MOVEMENT_BALANCE_SECTION,
  SEDENTARY_EXTRA,
} from "../../data/science";
---

<div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="Movement Balance" /></div>
<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <MovementBalance />
</div>
<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {[...MOVEMENT_BALANCE_SECTION, ...SEDENTARY_EXTRA].map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>

<h3 id="what-each-figure-on-the-card-means" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">What each figure on the card means</h3>
<div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Sedentary hours.</strong> A waking hour with fewer than
    50 steps in it counts as sedentary. Steps come from Apple Health, so nothing is typed in.
    Waking hours are your measured sleep where Verve has it, and 7am to 10pm where it does
    not — the card says which, because an assumed wake time counts somebody's actual lie-in
    as sitting.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Unbroken sitting.</strong> The longest run of
    consecutive sedentary hours, not the daily total. This is the figure the evidence is
    actually about: risk tracks uninterrupted sitting rather than hours accumulated across a
    day, and six hours broken every thirty minutes is a different day from six hours in one
    block. A sleep hour or a workout ends a run — you were not sitting through either.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Standing minutes.</strong> Minutes Apple Watch detected
    you upright and moving. More precise than the Stand ring, which credits a whole hour for
    one minute on your feet: this counts the minutes themselves. Apple Watch only.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Napped.</strong> Daytime sleep, shown when there was
    some. A nap is any sleep session that does not begin in the night (20:00 to 05:00),
    however long it runs. It is drawn as rest on the terrain and excluded from sitting — you
    were not sitting through it — and it is excluded from last night's sleep on the Sleep
    card, because a nap is not a bedtime. The same rule decides both.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">The 24-hour terrain.</strong> Height is steps per hour.
    The strip beneath it is the hour's state: green active, orange sedentary, blue asleep
    (naps included), grey an hour that has not happened yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Longest sit, and sits over 30 minutes.</strong>
    Uninterrupted sitting, detected from five-minute step buckets rather than hours &mdash;
    the resolution the evidence needs. Diaz found that mortality tracked not only total
    sedentary time but how it accumulated: the excess sat with bouts of thirty minutes or
    more, and was highest in people whose sitting came in blocks of an hour or longer. A bout
    is closed by any movement, by a recorded workout (a swim registers no steps and would
    otherwise read as the day&rsquo;s longest sit), and by sleep, naps included. Under the
    terrain a thin row marks each one &mdash; amber to an hour, red past it &mdash; so the
    figure above can be read off the day rather than trusted. Verve had this once on hourly
    steps and removed it, because an hourly total cannot see a thirty-minute bout; it returned
    only when the data could support it, and on a day without fine buckets it shows nothing
    rather than zero.
  </p>
</div>
